WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.000
- Hello.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.170
Good afternoon, I'm Alice Kaplan,

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.780
Director of the Whitney Humanities Center.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.700
Thank you to the Department of French

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.330
for joining the Whitney as a co-sponsor

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:24.747
of Juliette Cherbuliez's
"Medea: A Manifesto."

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:27.980
Juliette Cherbuliez is the Director

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.270
of the Center for Premodern Studies

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:34.022
and a professor of French at
the University of Minnesota.

00:00:34.022 --> 00:00:37.430
A comparatist, she's
published on the materiality

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:40.960
and politics of women's
writing in early modern France,

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.670
tragedy, violence, and visual culture.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.030
Her most recent book

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:49.307
interrogates these concepts at their core.

00:00:49.307 --> 00:00:51.150
"In the Wake of Medea:

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:55.240
Tragedy and the Arts of
Destruction in Neoclassical France"

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:58.300
is at the heart of her talk today.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.017
I want to mention too her previous book,

00:01:01.017 --> 00:01:02.840
"The Place of Exile:

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.000
Leisure Literature and
the Limits of Absolutism."

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.190
Here, she's concerned with writers

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:12.580
who are not considered
explicitly political,

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.410
banished noblewomen,
novelists, and poor refugees

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:19.600
who sought out a space
for writing and living

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.860
beyond Louis XIV's absolutist reach.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:25.640
In both of these books,

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.990
Juliette explores what she calls

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.600
the lost opportunities of
the early modern period,

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.840
concepts, themes, ideas

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.050
that did not become part of our modernity.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:41.150
She asks questions that
challenge many commonplaces,

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.380
that look at what has been overlooked,

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.530
that revolutionize our point of view

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:48.250
on concepts we think we know.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:50.750
The political, for example.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.340
Through her original
and powerful readings,

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.630
Juliette Cherbuliez shows

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.050
that figures of the early modern period

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.900
are essential to our own thought,

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.970
and at the same time, she
underscores their otherness,

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:07.663
so that we not project our
own categories onto them.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:10.370
Before we welcome Juliette,

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:14.440
please note that she'll be back
with us this coming Monday,

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:17.700
February 21st at 4:30

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.420
as part of the "Uprooting
Medea" presentation

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.050
featuring visiting artist Shivaike Shah,

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:28.870
who will discuss his production
of Euripides' Greek tragedy

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:32.190
with an all-multicultural cast and crew.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:35.160
Shiv will be in
conversation with Juliette,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.350
along with Yale faculty
Christina Kraus in Classics

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:42.500
and Eric Glover in the School of Drama.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:47.050
And we're putting a
registration link in the chat.

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:48.510
After Juliette's talk,

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:51.880
we'll have a few minutes for
questions from the audience,

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:56.230
so please put your questions
in the Q and A panel,

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.460
and feel free to submit your questions

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.610
at any time during the talk.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:04.710
Juliette, welcome to the
virtual French department

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.490
and Whitney Humanities Center.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:08.783
Let's get started.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:14.210
- Alice, thank you so much
for that warm introduction

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:18.770
and to the invitation from
the Whitney Humanities Center.

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:21.650
I also heartily thank Pierre Saint-Amand

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:24.333
and your colleagues in
the French department.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:27.790
While it is really unfortunate for me

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:29.840
that I am not in New Haven today,

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.210
this virtual encounter recalls to me

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:35.720
times really not that long ago

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:40.720
of mobility, conviviality, and
shared intellectual projects.

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:43.530
In fact, my last professional trip,

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.550
weeks before the pandemic hit,

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:48.510
involved work with
Professor Maurice Samuels.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:51.860
My last international
conference was in Fribourg,

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:54.625
organized by Professor Christophe Schuwey,

00:03:54.625 --> 00:03:57.517
and so in so many ways, this
talk marks for me anyway,

00:03:57.517 --> 00:03:58.820
a (speaks in foreign language),

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:02.260
a taking back up of
conversations forestalled.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.520
There's one intellectual exchange

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:08.240
which not even a global
calamity could stop,

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:09.850
and that is the one I've had

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:12.350
with Associate Director of
the Whitney Humanities Center

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:13.830
Diane Berrett Brown,

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:16.160
and for her contributions
realizing this event,

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:18.140
I am deeply grateful,

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:21.410
but for her generosity,
grace, and unending smarts

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:25.120
that have informed my work
for nearly two decades,

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:26.350
I'm really indebted.

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:29.090
So, it's a real pleasure to be with you,

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:30.440
with all of you here today.

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:33.670
I hope you can see my screen.

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:35.620
The images are pretty key to this talk.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:42.380
There is a type of news
photo that has haunted me,

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:47.380
perhaps you too, for about
the past five, six years.

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:50.560
This one might be the
most amazing example,

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:52.730
but there are many iterations
of this composition

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:55.290
and the confrontation it records.

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:57.470
They capture events all over,

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:02.470
from Chile in 2016, to Moscow in 2019,

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:07.560
to my adopted city of Minneapolis
from just two summers ago,

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.000
and again this past summer.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.620
There are other more
bureaucratic versions of this.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.920
They represent a type, that
of a girl or young woman

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.040
standing up to institutional power

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.270
in North America and elsewhere,
especially the Global South,

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:24.120
often a black or brown girl.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:28.240
So often a young woman not
from a dominating class.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.490
We have been taken by
surprise for a long time

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:34.970
by the reality of girl power

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:40.230
as an ideal of political contestation,

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:44.400
but also of gendered
resiliency as we can see

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.360
in the astonishing assertion

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:48.220
that "Females are strong as hell,"

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:49.780
to quote the title sequence

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:52.360
from Netflix's "Unbreakable
Kimmy Schmidt,"

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:53.920
a show which when you think about it

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.670
explores how PTSD from
15 years of kidnapping,

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:00.230
brainwashing, misogyny, and abuse

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:03.373
can help a young woman from the
Midwest make it in New York.

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:08.880
I do understand why we want
heroes, ideal people of action.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:12.600
What makes these people
particularly heroic?

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:14.700
Generally, we don't know or ask

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:16.500
what happens after these photos.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.263
It's the stance of defiance
itself that we admire.

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:23.470
I can't help but come back
to this one of Ieshia Evans,

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:26.170
which displays the
contradictions of an encounter

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:29.760
between the older, seemingly
older, versus the younger,

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:32.370
the overly weaponized
powerful and protected

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.720
versus the unarmed and vulnerable,

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:36.760
the many versus the singular.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:39.000
It is the stillness and strength,

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.760
that resilient statuesque
position here of Ieshia Evans

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:48.020
against the seemingly
choreographed dance of power.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:51.363
It is that which I call
the Antigone effect.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.950
Antigone has been the
darling of political theory

00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:57.380
since arguably the 18th century.

00:06:57.380 --> 00:07:00.270
She is, fitting to our story of modernity,

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:02.090
an icon of political hope,

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:04.720
even as she embodies tragic failure

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:07.500
from her obstinate and fearless conviction

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:10.710
that she must do right by
her rebel brother's corpse

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:13.600
and bury it in the face
of a royal interdiction,

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:15.880
through her self-generated peerlessness

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:18.760
as she refuses accomplices or partners,

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:22.187
especially her truth-telling
in the king's face.

00:07:22.187 --> 00:07:24.467
"Yes," Antigone says to Creon,

00:07:24.467 --> 00:07:26.640
"I did it. I am guilty."

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.313
And finally, most poignantly,

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:32.490
to her lament about her foreclosed future

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:35.160
before she is taken away to be walled up,

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:38.380
where she is found having hanged herself.

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:41.850
Antigone is constituted as
a paragon of the individual,

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:45.810
whose allegiances to her
particular familial sacred duties

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:49.713
compel her to test the state's
power and deny its hegemony.

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:51.610
There's something surely here

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:53.760
about the pure potential
of youth, to be sure.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:56.887
If you do attempt to do an
internet search of, for example,

00:07:56.887 --> 00:08:00.770
"old women resist power," or
"old women against police,"

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:04.800
or "old woman as icon of
resistance," you don't find much.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:08.103
If you try really, really hard,
you come up with this one.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.360
Note that she is walking away.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:14.320
My favorite part of this
is actually the handbag.

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:16.690
But it can't all be about youth.

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:19.810
The Antigone effect I refer
to owes its enduring appeal,

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:22.760
it seems, rather to a complex of features.

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:24.760
To try another tack at that effect,

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:28.210
and with a nod to the central
subject of my remarks today,

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:31.210
a search for an analogous Medea effect

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:33.033
yields very different results.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:37.763
This is Peter Ward's 2009 book,

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:41.050
asserts that life on Earth

00:08:41.050 --> 00:08:43.840
tends towards simplicity, not complexity,

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:46.920
so involves fundamentally
self destruction.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:49.560
Medea's also the name of
a gene in a fruit fly,

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:51.300
and as an acronym, it's also the name

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:53.710
of an effect of another gene,

00:08:53.710 --> 00:08:56.540
which has both a toxin and its antidote.

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:58.880
So those of you familiar
with Medea's story

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:01.707
know why these results concern

00:09:01.707 --> 00:09:06.180
the fundamental act of a
life-giver taking life.

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:09.830
Euripides made her famous for
having killed her children,

00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:13.650
but a full transhistorical
consideration of Medea

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:15.320
must however include

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:18.590
how she also betrays
her father and kingdom,

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:20.850
hacks her brothers into pieces,

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:23.220
causes the death of her husband's uncle

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:25.230
at the hands of his own daughters,

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:28.590
destroys a city having caused
the poisoned burning deaths

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:31.260
of its king and princess.

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:35.470
Despite, and maybe because of
all these gruesome actions,

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:39.400
I am interested in what is to
be gained by offering Medea

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:42.703
the same attention we
have lavished on Antigone.

00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:46.933
If you came to this talk
from the Schwarzman Center,

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:48.893
from the advertisement,

00:09:48.893 --> 00:09:50.860
I shall say that you may
be a little disappointed,

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:53.940
because I am certainly
not here to promote Medea

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:56.940
as a salutary icon of positive action,

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:00.250
but I am deeply interested in this figure

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:04.313
as a political figure,
and a figure of politics.

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:08.610
And what follows comes
from my most recent book,

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:10.970
which treats the specific case of violence

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:12.180
in the 17th century

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:14.540
through the lens of what
I call Medean violence,

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:17.270
and I'll come to an example
from that book in a little bit,

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:20.250
but today I wanna discuss
how Antigone is conceived of

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:23.060
in our political imagination first,

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:28.060
and then I will return to
Medea, that "icon of horrorism,"

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:31.430
as philosopher Adriana
Cavarero has called her,

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:35.000
and why I think she might
be a more important model

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:36.573
for a future politics.

00:10:38.330 --> 00:10:40.370
Okay, so, Antigone and Medea

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:42.690
have one major aspect in common.

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:45.700
Each contests the authority of the state

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:48.340
at the time of its formation.

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:50.850
This is true in nearly every version.

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:52.750
Oh, here is my book.

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:54.730
I think we can move on from there, though.

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:57.610
This contestation of the
state at its formation

00:10:57.610 --> 00:11:00.230
is true in nearly every version,

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:02.843
and here are two of my favorites.

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:07.210
Of course, the details
of this contestation

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:09.710
and the warrants each makes
for her sense of justice

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:11.710
differ almost diametrically.

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:16.340
Antigone is a virgin who
grieves, contests, and dies.

00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:21.174
Medea is a mother who
travels, kills, and lives on.

00:11:21.174 --> 00:11:23.750
Antigone wishes to honor
her brother with a burial.

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:26.270
Medea chops her brother, Apsyrtus,

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:28.660
into bits to delay her father's pursuit,

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:32.490
compelling him to recover
the pieces for burial.

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:34.660
Antigone laments her father's curse,

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:36.420
whereas Medea carries with her

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:39.520
the knowledge bequeathed
to her through her house,

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:41.150
which includes not just the sun,

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:44.610
but masters of the
occult Circe and Hecate.

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:48.170
While, for example, this
is Judith Butler's argument

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:50.350
about promiscuous obedience,

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:53.180
Antigone challenges a
certain kind of family,

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:56.700
revealing the origins
of kingship and kinship

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:58.290
as both arbitrary,

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:01.393
Medea underscores precisely
the opposite logic,

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:04.160
the steadfast power of family,

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:08.313
whether chosen, Jason,
or imposed, her children.

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:10.240
Following this logic,

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:13.150
Medea traces the promise not of origins,

00:12:13.150 --> 00:12:15.870
but of the ambiguity of the future.

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:19.550
It is crucial that no matter
what destruction Medea wreaks,

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:23.130
she flies off alive, she's never killed,

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:26.110
to live on and perhaps then to return.

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:29.300
So while Antigone stands
for "a future interrupted,"

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:30.920
to borrow from Bonnie Honig,

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:34.170
Medea is an exemplar
at once of an archaism,

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:37.160
a kind of premodernity we cannot overcome,

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:40.610
and a future absolutely
guaranteed to arrive,

00:12:40.610 --> 00:12:43.063
but whose consequences we cannot foretell.

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:45.923
So, Antigone.

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:52.220
Exercising her private
familial and sacred task

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:54.780
of burying the dead against
the emergent ordering

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:56.890
of the force of the city,

00:12:56.890 --> 00:13:00.020
this young and unmarried
childless daughter of Oedipus

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:02.970
has come to represent
that force of domesticity,

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:05.100
the right of privacy, and the family

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:07.000
against the overreaching state,

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:09.380
the perils and potential
of individual resistance,

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:11.260
and, we shouldn't forget,

00:13:11.260 --> 00:13:13.810
the special kind of powerless power

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:15.440
of women in the modern state,

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:18.300
which has so often been a
test case for the individual

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:21.470
inalienable right to self-determination.

00:13:21.470 --> 00:13:26.200
But, has it been effective to
assign a young, aristocratic,

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:29.960
apparently-gendered female all our hopes?

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:32.720
It seems that Antigone has
become in our contemporary time

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:34.710
less a model of political action

00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:36.810
and more one of our inability

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:39.010
to marshal a clear and enduring vision

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:41.900
of what both the morality and
the politics of resistance

00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:43.050
should look like today.

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:47.310
Sophocles' play has
furnished us with the grounds

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:49.630
for imagining the
individual political subject

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:52.680
obeying laws that are
higher, older, and broader

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:53.820
than those of the state,

00:13:53.820 --> 00:13:56.260
even at that individual's peril.

00:13:56.260 --> 00:13:57.730
Antigone performs this function

00:13:57.730 --> 00:13:59.963
as early as the 18th century.

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:05.970
Consistent with Europe's
rumblings against monarchism

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:07.930
and toward liberal democracy,

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:09.180
Louis de Jaucourt,

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:11.930
the "Encyclopedie's"
most prolific contributor

00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:15.420
and an expert on legal
matters and jurisprudence,

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:18.440
even gives Antigone the last word

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:21.210
in his article on the political subject.

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:24.480
Refuting Hobbes's argument
about loyal subjects

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:26.350
working as instruments of the sovereign

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:27.930
and therefore bearing no responsibility

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:30.010
for the justice of their actions,

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:33.160
Jaucourt cites the
tragedy's most oft-cited

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:34.750
and famous scene,

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:38.650
the stichomythia standoff
between Antigone and Creon,

00:14:38.650 --> 00:14:41.260
and he ends his article with this.

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:43.220
So he gives Antigone the last word.

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:45.630
So from the mid 18th century on,

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:48.880
it is this duologue,
Antigone's challenge to

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:51.160
and refutation of Creon's authority

00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:53.190
over her private sacred duties,

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:54.830
which has become a touchstone

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:59.920
for Hegel, Lacan, Irigaray, Judith Butler,

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:02.383
and again, to a certain
extent, Bonnie Honig.

00:15:03.230 --> 00:15:05.940
As a scholar of performance and theater,

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:08.410
I find this move extracting Antigone

00:15:08.410 --> 00:15:11.100
from the network of
relations and interactions

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:12.880
which constitute her and her community

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:14.933
within the play reductive.

00:15:15.870 --> 00:15:18.260
Our ideal Antigone has become an Antigone

00:15:18.260 --> 00:15:20.900
who defies and contests,

00:15:20.900 --> 00:15:22.040
whereas in Sophocles' play,

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:26.520
she is also an Antigone
who primarily takes action

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:28.170
by burying her brother.

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:29.320
That is to say the tragedy,

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:31.210
even when driven by song and declamation

00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:33.330
as the art was for Sophocles
in the fifth century

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:34.920
before the Common Era,

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:36.710
is not just about its words.

00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:39.890
It is also about the
deeply material relations

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:42.550
and physical gestures that bind us.

00:15:42.550 --> 00:15:45.210
Tragedy is by design and in performance

00:15:45.210 --> 00:15:48.160
neither monologic nor based on soliloquy,

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:50.820
not a medium of icons or figures,

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:52.420
but one in which it is precisely

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:54.530
the relations among characters,

00:15:54.530 --> 00:15:56.930
or the tensions among individual

00:15:56.930 --> 00:16:01.580
and collective significations
that are explored.

00:16:01.580 --> 00:16:04.230
The tragedy "Antigone" is, after all,

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:06.420
a play about people burying people.

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:08.500
The initial tension in the
play comes from a debate

00:16:08.500 --> 00:16:09.980
about who buried a corpse,

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:12.360
and whether one should bury a corpse.

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:14.240
The plummeting shock
at the end of the play,

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:15.600
when Antigone's own body

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:18.020
causes an avalanche of other deaths,

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:21.260
her beloved Haemon, who's also
Creon's son, kills himself,

00:16:21.260 --> 00:16:24.660
Haemon's mother the queen
then kills her herself,

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:28.090
which signals the fragility
of Creon's own family

00:16:28.090 --> 00:16:30.060
and therefore of the state.

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:32.660
This is an account of action after action.

00:16:32.660 --> 00:16:34.710
The best model for the tragedy Antigone

00:16:34.710 --> 00:16:35.870
is not the fight of words

00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:38.190
such as those between Creon and Antigone,

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:42.310
but the consequences of
individual actions on a community.

00:16:42.310 --> 00:16:43.470
While some have bemoaned

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:48.390
what they call the necropolitics
of the tragedy, "Antigone,"

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:50.450
sorry, of the tragedy "Antigone,"

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:53.800
what makes me most
nervous about its eponym,

00:16:53.800 --> 00:16:54.690
Antigone the character,

00:16:54.690 --> 00:16:57.930
is rather two other aspects
she's come to inhabit.

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:02.930
First, I am wary of the ways
we find Antigone hopeful,

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:06.500
despite her death, despite
the death she brings.

00:17:06.500 --> 00:17:08.820
Not only do I think this is a confusion

00:17:08.820 --> 00:17:11.390
of potential with efficacity,

00:17:11.390 --> 00:17:15.180
it also puts the burden of
future change on individuals,

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:17.040
as if the underlying structures,

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:20.580
the polis in which she
debates and maneuvers,

00:17:20.580 --> 00:17:22.370
to borrow from Bonnie Honig again,

00:17:22.370 --> 00:17:25.030
has always been constituted as such

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:26.963
and could never be anything but.

00:17:27.860 --> 00:17:29.440
My last complaint is this.

00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:33.110
How is it possible that
Antigone meets Creon one-on-one?

00:17:33.110 --> 00:17:36.530
It is astonishing that she might do so,

00:17:36.530 --> 00:17:39.150
because she's young,
because she's defiant,

00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:43.120
because she seems honest,
rigorous, uncompromising,

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:46.130
but it's because she is
Creon's future daughter-in-law,

00:17:46.130 --> 00:17:47.670
because she is his niece,

00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:50.340
because she is a member
of the House of Thebes,

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:51.940
because she is aristocratic

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:54.640
that she may stand to contest Creon's law,

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:56.360
and that she may bury her brother,

00:17:56.360 --> 00:17:59.980
and that she may marry
or even forsake Haemon.

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:02.730
Antigone is of the polis,
and she is of the family,

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:05.020
and it is by virtue of
these ancient rights,

00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:07.510
these innate entitlements, that she can,

00:18:07.510 --> 00:18:10.480
sacrificing herself, destabilize the city.

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:12.160
This points again to my discomfort

00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:14.970
with our appreciation of
all those media images

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:16.320
of mostly of women of color

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:18.900
standing strong in their resiliency.

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:21.320
These are not images of
how power really works.

00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:23.300
These are images many can appreciate

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:25.400
without suffering the consequences,

00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:28.340
either personally or structurally.

00:18:28.340 --> 00:18:31.510
What then, if our notion

00:18:31.510 --> 00:18:33.880
of the political subject
did not depart from

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:36.160
an aristocratic, youthful subject

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:38.210
who is of the reigning house?

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:40.730
Might we need to more radically

00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:43.100
redraw our political family tree

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:45.560
by considering the vagaries of geography,

00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:48.853
conquest, inheritance, and affiliation?

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:52.730
To do so might compel
us to reconsider who,

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:55.980
and whose stories, constitute
the House of Thebes,

00:18:55.980 --> 00:18:58.730
and who has been excluded from the polls

00:18:58.730 --> 00:19:01.210
as a feature of its constitution.

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:03.820
What if we contended
with the very conditions

00:19:03.820 --> 00:19:06.380
that the tragedy "Antigone" inlies?

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:10.140
After all, that tragedy
starts in the aftermath of war

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:12.770
and finishes with the regret
for a death not spared

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:14.483
and bodies strewn all about.

00:19:15.370 --> 00:19:18.230
What if the story of politics
included the statelessness

00:19:18.230 --> 00:19:21.100
that war brings home, the
persistence of revenge,

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:22.960
the state's monopoly of force,

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:26.573
and the problem of women who
do not die, but rather kill?

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:31.830
Or, to put it better, who
kill and yet do not die.

00:19:31.830 --> 00:19:34.710
In sum, the question
of the role of violence

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:37.133
in the formation and
maintenance of society.

00:19:39.400 --> 00:19:42.970
Appropriate to her final entombment,

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:46.647
Antigone's name means, as
Anne Carson reminds us,

00:19:46.647 --> 00:19:50.870
"against birth," or,
"instead of being born."

00:19:50.870 --> 00:19:52.640
We might hope that Medea's name

00:19:52.640 --> 00:19:54.330
might mean "against the mother,"

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:56.790
or, "instead of childbearing,"

00:19:56.790 --> 00:19:59.570
since she has been so
intimately associated

00:19:59.570 --> 00:20:01.780
with mothers who are selfish

00:20:01.780 --> 00:20:05.230
and who kill their offsprings
or otherwise eat life.

00:20:05.230 --> 00:20:07.220
And while Antigone's final words

00:20:07.220 --> 00:20:09.910
are a lament about never
marrying or having children,

00:20:09.910 --> 00:20:14.330
Medea's major lament decries
the pain of childbirth,

00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:17.740
and contrasts it with the
trivial wounds of war.

00:20:17.740 --> 00:20:19.967
Medea's name means rather

00:20:19.967 --> 00:20:23.057
"intelligent planner," or "contriver."

00:20:24.410 --> 00:20:26.930
It derives from the
Indo-European root "med-"

00:20:26.930 --> 00:20:29.877
which Emile Benveniste defines as

00:20:29.877 --> 00:20:33.190
"to take authoritatively those measures

00:20:33.190 --> 00:20:35.040
which are appropriate."

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:37.500
The first syllable of
Medea shares the same root

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:39.883
as meditate or medicine.

00:20:40.775 --> 00:20:42.550
Medea's careful deployment of knowledge

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:45.080
names the same authoritative care

00:20:45.080 --> 00:20:48.620
as that informing then
deep study or healing.

00:20:48.620 --> 00:20:52.830
This is why she is so
often portrayed as a witch.

00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:54.380
This ambiguity might explain

00:20:54.380 --> 00:20:56.500
why the most common
visual depiction of her

00:20:56.500 --> 00:20:58.490
in premodern iconography

00:20:58.490 --> 00:21:01.653
is the rejuvenation of
Aeson, Jason's father.

00:21:02.780 --> 00:21:06.690
Or, is it that of Medea

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:09.960
killing Jason's usurping uncle Pelias?

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:12.780
The gestures are similar
enough to give one pause,

00:21:12.780 --> 00:21:14.370
and to make explicit the connection

00:21:14.370 --> 00:21:15.930
between healing and killing.

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:19.390
You can hear here echos of
Jacques Derrida's pharmakon

00:21:19.390 --> 00:21:22.170
as the medium for
language's undecidability.

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:25.680
So the pharmakis, the
wizard, the magician pioneer

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:28.780
always encounters its homophone pharmakos,

00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:32.820
that exiled, sacrificed
scapegoat sent out of the city

00:21:32.820 --> 00:21:36.080
and ritually killed as a purification and,

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:38.500
as Derrida says, a remedy

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:42.530
for the polis.

00:21:42.530 --> 00:21:44.320
Medea is both at once.

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:46.910
She is exiled from her
own land of Colchis,

00:21:46.910 --> 00:21:48.510
so a foreigner in Corinth.

00:21:48.510 --> 00:21:50.350
She will also fly away from Corinth

00:21:50.350 --> 00:21:52.810
and be a foreigner wherever she goes.

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:55.560
She has knowledge that is
deemed unnatural to the Greeks

00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:57.520
because it is foreign and potent.

00:21:57.520 --> 00:22:02.520
Her estranged position is
geopolitical and epistemological.

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:04.580
In Medea's actions,

00:22:04.580 --> 00:22:07.520
the knowledge of healing
and killing are linked,

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:10.513
as are magician and exile.

00:22:12.890 --> 00:22:16.430
Two likenesses of Medea speak volumes

00:22:16.430 --> 00:22:18.840
about this confluence
of knowledge and exile.

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:20.430
They're both on Roman sarcophagi

00:22:20.430 --> 00:22:22.480
from the mid to late second century.

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:26.570
Both show Medea depicted
as herbalist or arsonist,

00:22:26.570 --> 00:22:29.070
connecting the science
of knowing and making

00:22:29.070 --> 00:22:33.343
with the elemental reality of
combustion and distribution.

00:22:34.180 --> 00:22:36.160
Each depicts the story of Jason and Medea

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:38.240
from the theft of the Golden Fleece

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:39.730
to the death of the children,

00:22:39.730 --> 00:22:44.200
and the story is told
on three or four sides,

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:45.550
I'm forgetting now, but it,

00:22:47.235 --> 00:22:51.837
it follows the sides of the sarcophagus.

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:57.650
And you can see right in
the middle of this one,

00:22:57.650 --> 00:23:00.500
you can see an amalgam
of both of these crimes,

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:03.710
the theft of the Fleece and
the death of the children,

00:23:03.710 --> 00:23:05.940
with the death of Creusa,

00:23:05.940 --> 00:23:07.650
which I'll point out using my cursor,

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:10.230
which I hope you can
see, right in the middle,

00:23:10.230 --> 00:23:12.840
the death of Creusa after having donned

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:17.840
a poisoned robe gifted
by Medea for her wedding.

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:21.000
This first one is at the
Basel Museum of Antiquities.

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:22.280
You can see on the left

00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:26.760
the children bringing the
robe to the bride Creusa,

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:28.263
who will be marrying Jason.

00:23:29.710 --> 00:23:33.940
You can see on the right
Medea in her chariot,

00:23:33.940 --> 00:23:38.763
flying away with a child
slung over her shoulders.

00:23:40.593 --> 00:23:42.670
And the other one is in there too.

00:23:42.670 --> 00:23:46.460
At the center, Creusa as if in movement,

00:23:46.460 --> 00:23:47.610
is as if in movement,

00:23:47.610 --> 00:23:50.600
her own hand stretched
out in front of her.

00:23:50.600 --> 00:23:52.100
She's running away from the gown

00:23:52.100 --> 00:23:54.470
that clings to and will kill her.

00:23:54.470 --> 00:23:57.130
Her father cries out in back of her.

00:23:57.130 --> 00:24:02.130
And underneath her left
knee is a box or a stool,

00:24:02.280 --> 00:24:05.150
ordained with tragic masks.

00:24:05.150 --> 00:24:08.663
Even more discreetly, behind
the two children to her right,

00:24:09.720 --> 00:24:13.030
there is another box, hardly
visible in this image,

00:24:13.030 --> 00:24:16.330
but art historians of Rome confirm,

00:24:16.330 --> 00:24:18.470
it is a metonym for the absent Medea,

00:24:18.470 --> 00:24:21.573
it is the case in which
she keeps her herbs.

00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:25.410
The second sarcophagus,

00:24:25.410 --> 00:24:28.490
now at Berlin's new museum of antiquities,

00:24:28.490 --> 00:24:30.430
is sparser than Basel's,

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:32.670
so we're even more immediately drawn

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:34.850
to the Creon-Creusa couple,

00:24:34.850 --> 00:24:37.720
who, despite the available space,

00:24:37.720 --> 00:24:41.070
this sarcophagus is much less
populated than the other one,

00:24:41.070 --> 00:24:44.750
despite that available space
are even closer together here,

00:24:44.750 --> 00:24:49.240
his curly beard nearly
touching her flowing locks.

00:24:49.240 --> 00:24:50.960
The power of Medea is expressed

00:24:50.960 --> 00:24:52.850
not by such accessories as boxes,

00:24:52.850 --> 00:24:55.780
but through the metamorphosis
of Creusa's hair,

00:24:55.780 --> 00:24:57.150
that symbol of virginity,

00:24:57.150 --> 00:25:00.393
and often as it flows before
marriage and shearing, youth,

00:25:01.320 --> 00:25:04.700
into, sorry, a symbol of femininity,

00:25:04.700 --> 00:25:06.530
and often before shearing in marriage,

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:08.520
the symbol of youth and virginity,

00:25:08.520 --> 00:25:12.790
and you can see that Creusa's
hair is turning into flames.

00:25:12.790 --> 00:25:15.850
Part of it, here's a closeup,

00:25:15.850 --> 00:25:19.470
part of it reaches skyward,
touching the frame,

00:25:19.470 --> 00:25:21.063
as does Creusa's hand.

00:25:22.041 --> 00:25:24.690
Medea's actions, whether
she's depicted as an herbalist

00:25:24.690 --> 00:25:25.770
or an arsonist,

00:25:25.770 --> 00:25:28.270
connect the science of knowing and making

00:25:28.270 --> 00:25:32.193
with the elemental reality of
combustion and destruction.

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:36.420
I have wondered a lot

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:38.610
about why I continue to think alongside

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:41.513
a figure of such destruction
and calculated harm.

00:25:42.610 --> 00:25:45.320
It comes back to the now classic analysis

00:25:45.320 --> 00:25:49.860
offered by Bruno Latour in the early 1990s

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:53.510
regarding what he calls
the modern constitution,

00:25:53.510 --> 00:25:57.180
that agreement made to
distinguish absolutely

00:25:57.180 --> 00:26:00.870
between nature and society,
between humans and non-humans.

00:26:00.870 --> 00:26:04.070
Medea thematizes a violation
of this modern constitution,

00:26:04.070 --> 00:26:05.360
or perhaps she is simply,

00:26:05.360 --> 00:26:08.283
as I argue in the book, always premodern.

00:26:09.280 --> 00:26:11.090
Her very old and foreign knowledge

00:26:11.090 --> 00:26:13.440
inherited from the sun
is what confirms her

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:15.830
as an interloper in the Greek world,

00:26:15.830 --> 00:26:17.810
who cannot be assimilated.

00:26:17.810 --> 00:26:20.480
That reminds us that
Medea is on the outside,

00:26:20.480 --> 00:26:24.870
both of a Latourian constitution
and that of the polis.

00:26:24.870 --> 00:26:26.290
Medea is doubly foreign.

00:26:26.290 --> 00:26:27.500
She comes from another place,

00:26:27.500 --> 00:26:29.540
but she also imports and mobilizes

00:26:29.540 --> 00:26:31.530
another way of ordering the world.

00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:32.970
This is what I've come to think of

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:35.410
as epistemological alterity,

00:26:35.410 --> 00:26:38.940
when what and how one
knows contravenes the laws

00:26:38.940 --> 00:26:40.750
of nature and the polis,

00:26:40.750 --> 00:26:43.363
such as the locals have established them.

00:26:44.350 --> 00:26:47.653
With epistemological alterity
there is no assimilation.

00:26:48.930 --> 00:26:51.140
A quick example from
the many I might offer

00:26:51.140 --> 00:26:53.240
from the theater of 17th century France,

00:26:53.240 --> 00:26:54.960
that most classic of centuries,

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:58.690
according to traditional
canon and periodization,

00:26:58.690 --> 00:27:03.340
the apogee of absolutism
where order reigned so supreme

00:27:03.340 --> 00:27:06.010
that even tragedy
eventually was disciplined

00:27:07.599 --> 00:27:10.670
within an inch of its
aesthetic life, as it were.

00:27:10.670 --> 00:27:13.160
Strict conventions came to prohibit

00:27:13.160 --> 00:27:16.860
any display of passion
on the tragic stage,

00:27:16.860 --> 00:27:18.010
as a matter of decency.

00:27:19.870 --> 00:27:23.730
Thus, when Pierre Corneille
oversees the publication

00:27:23.730 --> 00:27:26.280
of his complete works in 1660,

00:27:26.280 --> 00:27:30.010
each play is accompanied not
by a representation of a scene

00:27:30.010 --> 00:27:31.580
that was acted onstage,

00:27:31.580 --> 00:27:34.300
but ones that were of such violence

00:27:34.300 --> 00:27:37.360
that they were recounted and described,

00:27:37.360 --> 00:27:38.960
but not actually played out.

00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:41.850
So here on the left from "Horace,"

00:27:41.850 --> 00:27:44.490
the battle scene between
the Horaces and the Curiaces

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:46.910
exemplifies this editorial strategy.

00:27:46.910 --> 00:27:50.270
It's reported three times in the play,

00:27:50.270 --> 00:27:53.690
as if happening just offstage.

00:27:53.690 --> 00:27:57.010
The tragedy of "Medee," on the right,

00:27:57.010 --> 00:27:58.970
is the exception to this rule.

00:27:58.970 --> 00:28:01.130
This is the final scene where Medee,

00:28:01.130 --> 00:28:03.120
having destroyed the city
and killed her children,

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:04.570
is riding off in her chariot,

00:28:04.570 --> 00:28:06.880
and Jason is left on
the ground in Corinth.

00:28:06.880 --> 00:28:09.600
I could talk a lot about why
Corneille and his printers

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:12.470
deviated from this program
and printed a scene

00:28:12.470 --> 00:28:16.720
that is actually played
in the play itself,

00:28:16.720 --> 00:28:19.020
but instead I wanna
return to another image

00:28:19.020 --> 00:28:20.423
of this same scene.

00:28:21.650 --> 00:28:25.033
This one is from 1664.

00:28:27.670 --> 00:28:32.170
It is from another complete edition

00:28:32.170 --> 00:28:35.340
of the theater of Pierre Corneille,

00:28:35.340 --> 00:28:37.490
and I'm just gonna blow
it up a little bit.

00:28:38.530 --> 00:28:43.170
It is borrowing from the
1660 edition in composition,

00:28:43.170 --> 00:28:44.980
but certainly not in technique.

00:28:44.980 --> 00:28:48.430
You maybe you can see that
it's much less capable,

00:28:48.430 --> 00:28:51.773
not as nice detail, not as well printed.

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:56.760
This is an unauthorized
version of Corneille's work

00:28:56.760 --> 00:28:59.010
printed and sold in Amsterdam.

00:28:59.010 --> 00:29:01.600
This is an addition that for centuries,,

00:29:01.600 --> 00:29:02.900
up until decades ago,

00:29:02.900 --> 00:29:05.993
was routinely omitted from
scholarly bibliographies

00:29:05.993 --> 00:29:07.180
of Corneille's work,

00:29:07.180 --> 00:29:09.300
since it was considered a counterfeit,

00:29:09.300 --> 00:29:11.530
despite that term being both

00:29:11.530 --> 00:29:14.970
completely anachronistic and inaccurate.

00:29:14.970 --> 00:29:18.880
So 19th and 20th century
scholars perpetuated in some ways

00:29:18.880 --> 00:29:22.910
the 17th century's own borders,

00:29:22.910 --> 00:29:26.990
as if a Dutch printer could
not print a French text,

00:29:26.990 --> 00:29:31.280
or if he did so was in a
certain way transgressive

00:29:31.280 --> 00:29:33.280
or in excess.

00:29:33.280 --> 00:29:34.500
Let's look more carefully

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:36.780
at some of the other
excesses of this image.

00:29:36.780 --> 00:29:40.030
Let me see if I can get there.

00:29:40.030 --> 00:29:43.420
Jason is still stage right,

00:29:43.420 --> 00:29:47.150
while further upstage two
bodies remain visible.

00:29:47.150 --> 00:29:48.780
One is on the ground.

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:52.160
The forms are indistinct
so that they are not meant,

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:54.020
so who they're meant to
be can only be inferred.

00:29:54.020 --> 00:29:56.920
Medee is in her dragon-drawn chariot,

00:29:56.920 --> 00:29:59.030
although this one is
drawn by someone else.

00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:01.870
What is remarkably different in this image

00:30:01.870 --> 00:30:03.880
is the prominence of destruction.

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:07.000
We can see that the clouds
bearing the chariot,

00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:08.620
that appear in both images,

00:30:08.620 --> 00:30:12.840
in the Amsterdam images are clearly clouds

00:30:12.840 --> 00:30:14.800
from the burning building.

00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:16.760
And in fact, the destruction in this image

00:30:16.760 --> 00:30:19.090
is far more explicit.

00:30:19.090 --> 00:30:21.260
And let's see if I can
get a closeup there.

00:30:21.260 --> 00:30:22.490
There we go.

00:30:22.490 --> 00:30:26.233
Corinth is not only still
shown to be burning,

00:30:27.360 --> 00:30:31.050
flames escape the palace's orifices,

00:30:31.050 --> 00:30:33.900
but even a skeleton peeks out

00:30:33.900 --> 00:30:37.300
from the fire above the doorway, grinning,

00:30:37.300 --> 00:30:39.920
perhaps at the witnesses who are pointing,

00:30:39.920 --> 00:30:41.970
who are standing below pointing upward,

00:30:41.970 --> 00:30:44.183
whether to it or to Medee.

00:30:45.200 --> 00:30:48.420
The Amsterdam image is an apt

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:51.773
symbol for Medean violence.

00:30:51.773 --> 00:30:55.250
A non-authorized version
created and circulated

00:30:55.250 --> 00:30:59.440
just beyond its author's
sphere of influence

00:30:59.440 --> 00:31:02.310
and also outside of the
confines of the monarchy

00:31:02.310 --> 00:31:04.900
for which classical tragedy
would be set to work.

00:31:04.900 --> 00:31:07.220
It also figures the scale, scope,

00:31:07.220 --> 00:31:09.110
and quality of Medean violence,

00:31:09.110 --> 00:31:11.423
and its legacy in and beyond Corneille.

00:31:12.290 --> 00:31:14.750
Much like this Amsterdam
image, for many critics,

00:31:14.750 --> 00:31:17.000
the violence of this
myth is explained in part

00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:18.680
by the constitution of its eponym.

00:31:18.680 --> 00:31:23.170
Medee the character remains
an outsider, unassimilable,

00:31:23.170 --> 00:31:26.060
whether of female power

00:31:26.060 --> 00:31:29.390
or of non-European
cultures and knowledges.

00:31:30.410 --> 00:31:34.363
While Antigone stands inside
or outside of the city,

00:31:35.780 --> 00:31:40.170
the figure of Medea has no
warrant to be wherever it is.

00:31:40.170 --> 00:31:45.053
Here again she is, children in tow,

00:31:46.080 --> 00:31:47.600
the dragon's snakelike body

00:31:47.600 --> 00:31:49.620
curling through the chariot's wheel.

00:31:49.620 --> 00:31:52.633
Regular technology is
useless for this flight.

00:31:53.470 --> 00:31:57.290
The snake's sinuating catch in the wheels

00:31:57.290 --> 00:31:59.540
will also remind me that while Antigone

00:31:59.540 --> 00:32:02.230
remains peerless and
absolute in her conviction,

00:32:02.230 --> 00:32:04.550
Medea has no moral purity to stand on

00:32:04.550 --> 00:32:06.543
and so offers no moral lesson.

00:32:09.170 --> 00:32:10.170
Always a foreigner,

00:32:10.170 --> 00:32:13.230
always carrying with and carried
by its foreign knowledge,

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:15.870
the figure of Medea inserts
itself into our community,

00:32:15.870 --> 00:32:17.630
whether we want it there or not.

00:32:17.630 --> 00:32:19.590
And while Antigone dies alone,

00:32:19.590 --> 00:32:21.963
Medea lives, kills, and lives on.

00:32:22.860 --> 00:32:27.420
Medea's modus operandi is a
cleaving to and from others,

00:32:27.420 --> 00:32:29.970
a politics of attachments and detachment,

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:32.350
which offers a radical model,

00:32:32.350 --> 00:32:35.870
ordering politics according
to relations among people,

00:32:35.870 --> 00:32:38.900
not by vesting the individual
in its own license.

00:32:38.900 --> 00:32:41.580
Finally then, Medea indicates the extent

00:32:41.580 --> 00:32:43.230
to which our political order,

00:32:43.230 --> 00:32:46.980
liberal democracy based on
self-possessed individualism,

00:32:46.980 --> 00:32:50.180
is predicated on the exclusion
not just of certain actors,

00:32:50.180 --> 00:32:51.763
but of certain knowledges.

00:32:53.500 --> 00:32:55.950
Oh, I said I would offer you a manifesto,

00:32:55.950 --> 00:32:58.160
and I think I've only done so in part.

00:32:58.160 --> 00:33:00.520
The problem is, what now?

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:04.490
I am not standing here
on a rhetorical rampart

00:33:04.490 --> 00:33:06.350
urging you to follow me.

00:33:06.350 --> 00:33:08.510
Medea has, I've said several times,

00:33:08.510 --> 00:33:11.130
no lesson to offer, and nor do I.

00:33:11.130 --> 00:33:13.910
I maintain that liberal
democracy's celebration

00:33:13.910 --> 00:33:16.500
of the peerless individual
has let us astray,

00:33:16.500 --> 00:33:19.120
but if you were waiting
for me to torch this place

00:33:19.120 --> 00:33:20.940
and then kill my daughter,

00:33:20.940 --> 00:33:22.623
you have misread me entirely.

00:33:23.710 --> 00:33:26.540
Rather, I've tried to suggest
that the violence of Medea

00:33:26.540 --> 00:33:28.530
is in many ways here to stay

00:33:28.530 --> 00:33:30.110
because of how we have constituted

00:33:30.110 --> 00:33:32.152
both the city and its heroes.

00:33:32.152 --> 00:33:33.510
Medea's violence is also that

00:33:33.510 --> 00:33:35.330
of the figure's sense of history.

00:33:35.330 --> 00:33:36.670
It is relational.

00:33:36.670 --> 00:33:38.200
It is embedded.

00:33:38.200 --> 00:33:41.370
I think we need to seek a
politics that acknowledges

00:33:41.370 --> 00:33:42.990
that relational, that embedded,

00:33:42.990 --> 00:33:45.120
the fundamental injustice and untenability

00:33:45.120 --> 00:33:48.400
of a city carved out of the
included and the excluded.

00:33:48.400 --> 00:33:52.150
Perhaps also then we need
to emancipate ourselves,

00:33:52.150 --> 00:33:54.830
not just from giving all
our hopes to Antigone,

00:33:54.830 --> 00:33:57.210
but also to stop thinking
that we can imagine a world

00:33:57.210 --> 00:33:58.043
without any Medeas,

00:33:58.043 --> 00:34:01.370
or that the violence we know
that exists in the world

00:34:01.370 --> 00:34:06.073
can and should be kept
away, outside, over there.

00:34:07.260 --> 00:34:10.330
In nearly every version
of her tale, I've said,

00:34:10.330 --> 00:34:13.450
Medea flies away, never
captured or eliminated.

00:34:13.450 --> 00:34:16.710
If we built a politics
based on her sure return

00:34:16.710 --> 00:34:18.640
and her inevitable inclusion

00:34:18.640 --> 00:34:20.870
into our ways of being and knowing,

00:34:20.870 --> 00:34:23.460
what then would our cities look like?

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:24.293
Thank you.

00:34:28.220 --> 00:34:30.380
- Thank you so much.

00:34:30.380 --> 00:34:33.450
My goodness, I'm kind of, I'm reeling

00:34:33.450 --> 00:34:38.213
as I contemplate the incredible
paradox of your title.

00:34:40.300 --> 00:34:43.540
So I can't really ask you

00:34:43.540 --> 00:34:46.700
what this politics of
Medea would look like,

00:34:46.700 --> 00:34:50.700
but maybe I can ask you what we,

00:34:50.700 --> 00:34:55.420
and you might just be repeating
from the brilliant talk,

00:34:55.420 --> 00:34:57.180
but what do we lose?

00:34:57.180 --> 00:35:00.933
What do we lose when we
choose Antigone over Medea?

00:35:02.509 --> 00:35:05.470
- Oh, that's a really wonderful question.

00:35:05.470 --> 00:35:10.153
I think when we choose
Antigone over Medea,

00:35:11.440 --> 00:35:15.410
I think, I guess maybe I'm not
going to put it as a choice,

00:35:15.410 --> 00:35:18.493
if I can answer that question
a little differently.

00:35:19.620 --> 00:35:22.070
I think what happens when
we've chosen Antigone

00:35:22.070 --> 00:35:25.620
is we've caught ourselves
into a model that says

00:35:25.620 --> 00:35:30.583
political resistance takes the form,

00:35:31.630 --> 00:35:33.350
in some ways, of a sacrifice,

00:35:33.350 --> 00:35:38.350
and it costs the city very little

00:35:38.460 --> 00:35:41.510
to accept that as a form
of political resistance.

00:35:41.510 --> 00:35:43.890
- Right, 'cause the political agent

00:35:43.890 --> 00:35:46.400
has bumped themselves off, right?

00:35:46.400 --> 00:35:48.050
So no problem.
- Yes, yeah.

00:35:48.050 --> 00:35:50.920
Yes, and I think that we,

00:35:50.920 --> 00:35:54.400
I showed all those images
because I think it's so wonderful

00:35:54.400 --> 00:35:56.530
to see those images of defiance,

00:35:56.530 --> 00:36:00.060
but we're also trapping people in a bind,

00:36:00.060 --> 00:36:04.160
a bind that women of
color often point out,

00:36:04.160 --> 00:36:06.470
that their strength and their resiliency

00:36:06.470 --> 00:36:11.180
is seen as somehow
miraculous and superhuman,

00:36:11.180 --> 00:36:13.740
and yet situations don't change for them,

00:36:13.740 --> 00:36:15.210
and I think it's really important

00:36:15.210 --> 00:36:19.920
to complement that very
famous image from 2016

00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:22.620
of Ieshia Evans with the one
where she's being taken away,

00:36:22.620 --> 00:36:24.120
because she does get arrested.

00:36:25.010 --> 00:36:26.823
And I think this idea,

00:36:31.050 --> 00:36:36.050
this idea of Medea as a
heroine also puts an undue,

00:36:37.660 --> 00:36:39.710
the she's been constituted,
because I think this is,

00:36:39.710 --> 00:36:40.893
in part what I'm trying to suggest is

00:36:40.893 --> 00:36:44.080
there's a history in the
modern era about these figures

00:36:44.080 --> 00:36:46.264
that doesn't belong to the premodern era,

00:36:46.264 --> 00:36:49.750
and Antigone has been
constituted in the modern era

00:36:49.750 --> 00:36:52.270
as somebody who contests and debates,

00:36:52.270 --> 00:36:56.040
and I think it diminishes
the fact that she also acts,

00:36:56.040 --> 00:36:57.900
and that all tragedies,

00:36:57.900 --> 00:37:00.550
even when they're constituted by words,

00:37:00.550 --> 00:37:03.860
are also about the dilemmas
that our actions cause us.

00:37:03.860 --> 00:37:06.970
And that's where I think
Medea brings to the fore,

00:37:06.970 --> 00:37:08.400
so I guess I wouldn't,

00:37:08.400 --> 00:37:11.920
I've used Antigone as a
strawman of some kind,

00:37:11.920 --> 00:37:14.520
although I think that
the problem of Antigone

00:37:14.520 --> 00:37:15.750
is really important.

00:37:15.750 --> 00:37:17.670
So, I hope that

00:37:17.670 --> 00:37:19.090
answers something.
- Yeah no, that's very,

00:37:19.090 --> 00:37:20.300
it's very interesting,

00:37:20.300 --> 00:37:21.880
and I can see why you're resisting

00:37:21.880 --> 00:37:23.880
the one-on-one comparison.

00:37:23.880 --> 00:37:24.713
- Mmhm.

00:37:24.713 --> 00:37:26.733
- We've got a question here.

00:37:28.887 --> 00:37:32.297
"I'm intrigued by your claims about Medea

00:37:32.297 --> 00:37:35.090
and the steadfast power of family,

00:37:35.090 --> 00:37:37.760
even as she kills many of them,

00:37:37.760 --> 00:37:40.720
especially your take that
the status of the family

00:37:40.720 --> 00:37:43.770
speaks more to the ambiguity of the future

00:37:43.770 --> 00:37:45.610
than the power of origins.

00:37:45.610 --> 00:37:47.640
It's such a commonplace

00:37:47.640 --> 00:37:50.890
to think of family as
inextricable from origins.

00:37:50.890 --> 00:37:52.510
Could you say more about this move

00:37:52.510 --> 00:37:55.600
connecting Medea to an ambiguous future?

00:37:55.600 --> 00:37:58.370
'Manifesto' seemed very future-looking,

00:37:58.370 --> 00:38:00.720
so it seems like a key move."

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:03.200
- Yeah, and I did cop to the fact that

00:38:03.200 --> 00:38:07.420
I might have failed to
give you a manifesto, but,

00:38:07.420 --> 00:38:10.420
and that question is actually
really, really complicated,

00:38:10.420 --> 00:38:12.433
so maybe I'll take that first part,

00:38:13.320 --> 00:38:16.610
the claim about Medea and the
steadfast power of family,

00:38:16.610 --> 00:38:18.283
even if she kills many of them.

00:38:19.810 --> 00:38:22.820
So, one of the things I think that we

00:38:23.870 --> 00:38:27.690
perhaps don't appreciate as much...

00:38:28.780 --> 00:38:30.260
Let me answer this in a different way.

00:38:30.260 --> 00:38:32.860
I was just about to dig
myself into a terrible hole.

00:38:34.200 --> 00:38:38.140
So Medea, I will take an example of one of

00:38:39.584 --> 00:38:40.750
my most favorite versions of Medea,

00:38:40.750 --> 00:38:43.670
which is the Pier Paolo Pasolini version,

00:38:43.670 --> 00:38:46.150
where when Medea kills her children,

00:38:46.150 --> 00:38:50.710
she sings them a lullaby,
puts them to bed,

00:38:50.710 --> 00:38:53.910
and then kills them with a knife.

00:38:53.910 --> 00:38:57.640
And the scene is a scene of immense care,

00:38:57.640 --> 00:38:59.340
of immense tenderness.

00:38:59.340 --> 00:39:02.020
One doesn't quite see regret in her face.

00:39:02.020 --> 00:39:03.470
And if you haven't seen this movie,

00:39:03.470 --> 00:39:05.863
it's a really extraordinary movie.

00:39:08.190 --> 00:39:11.920
And so Medea, my claim about Medea

00:39:11.920 --> 00:39:15.790
is that Medea is very
tuned to those relations,

00:39:15.790 --> 00:39:16.820
and uses them.

00:39:16.820 --> 00:39:18.990
The word I use in the book is cleaving,

00:39:18.990 --> 00:39:21.420
cleaving to and cleaving from.

00:39:21.420 --> 00:39:25.280
She knows how to make people join

00:39:25.280 --> 00:39:27.190
who maybe should not be stuck together,

00:39:27.190 --> 00:39:31.000
so Creon and Creusa stick
together through the burning robe,

00:39:31.000 --> 00:39:34.380
Creon will eventually perish
from that same poisoned robe,

00:39:34.380 --> 00:39:37.623
and she also cleaves her
own children from herself.

00:39:38.480 --> 00:39:41.790
And what I mean to draw attention to

00:39:41.790 --> 00:39:46.790
with that analysis

00:39:46.980 --> 00:39:51.660
is that Medea pays attention
to our networked relations,

00:39:51.660 --> 00:39:54.583
and of course, family
is at the heart of that.

00:39:57.684 --> 00:40:02.684
So I think that family is
inextricable from origins,

00:40:02.760 --> 00:40:05.390
and I think Medea confirms that,

00:40:05.390 --> 00:40:09.500
but her uprootedness, her foreign status,

00:40:09.500 --> 00:40:13.813
also then brings to light the
question of the chosen family.

00:40:16.942 --> 00:40:18.510
The father of her children is Jason,

00:40:18.510 --> 00:40:21.760
and he chooses to marry somebody else.

00:40:21.760 --> 00:40:26.180
So where, what is the status of family?

00:40:26.180 --> 00:40:27.370
That's a problem,

00:40:27.370 --> 00:40:29.130
that's a problem in the
ancient world as well,

00:40:29.130 --> 00:40:30.610
as well as in the premodern world.

00:40:30.610 --> 00:40:34.630
So these are the kinds of
questions that the Medea myth asks

00:40:34.630 --> 00:40:38.160
that the Antigone myth
never, never needs to ask us.

00:40:38.160 --> 00:40:40.980
And in fact, Antigone refuses

00:40:40.980 --> 00:40:42.240
a relationship with her sister,

00:40:42.240 --> 00:40:44.120
she refuses complicity with her sister.

00:40:44.120 --> 00:40:46.160
So it's also about what's exacerbated,

00:40:46.160 --> 00:40:47.890
and, you know, what's put pressure,

00:40:47.890 --> 00:40:49.350
what the myths put pressure on

00:40:49.350 --> 00:40:53.227
and how our inventions of them
exacerbate those pressures.

00:40:53.227 --> 00:40:56.350
- Mm, mm. Okay.

00:40:56.350 --> 00:40:57.957
Another question.

00:40:57.957 --> 00:41:00.150
"Thank you so much for
this wonderful talk.

00:41:00.150 --> 00:41:02.510
So many questions. Here's one.

00:41:02.510 --> 00:41:06.470
Is there a way to measure
the actual effect,

00:41:06.470 --> 00:41:10.810
effectiveness, efficacy,
of Medea's representations

00:41:10.810 --> 00:41:12.210
on a given society?

00:41:12.210 --> 00:41:15.310
For example, is there a way to measure

00:41:15.310 --> 00:41:18.230
how performances of Corneille's 'Medea'

00:41:18.230 --> 00:41:20.880
transformed the sociopolitical fabric

00:41:20.880 --> 00:41:22.640
of early modern France?

00:41:22.640 --> 00:41:25.560
What kind of archives,
or type of evidence,

00:41:25.560 --> 00:41:28.253
or even method could we use?

00:41:29.140 --> 00:41:32.007
- Oh, my goodness, that's
such a hard question!

00:41:32.007 --> 00:41:35.080
(Juliette laughs)

00:41:35.080 --> 00:41:37.420
Well, the method I use in my book

00:41:37.420 --> 00:41:42.213
is to think about not Medea the myth,

00:41:43.300 --> 00:41:44.940
Medea the myth appears

00:41:44.940 --> 00:41:47.010
scattered throughout the 17th century,

00:41:47.010 --> 00:41:49.810
whereas there's very little Antigone.

00:41:49.810 --> 00:41:50.943
In fact, I'm,

00:41:52.310 --> 00:41:55.173
there's some Antigone, but not frontally,

00:41:56.210 --> 00:41:59.320
whereas Medea, but the term
that I used in the book

00:41:59.320 --> 00:42:03.060
is to think about this notion
of a persistence of violence

00:42:05.619 --> 00:42:08.730
that is, seems to be a rehearsal

00:42:08.730 --> 00:42:12.050
of the impossibility of violence
evacuation from society.

00:42:12.050 --> 00:42:16.020
So I take Medean violence
as a form of violence

00:42:16.020 --> 00:42:18.800
that I think is constitutive of society.

00:42:18.800 --> 00:42:21.600
So, how would I measure the effectiveness

00:42:21.600 --> 00:42:24.000
of Medea's representation
on a given society?

00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:26.783
Well, I don't think I would,

00:42:27.670 --> 00:42:31.380
I'm not sure that that's how
literary representations work,

00:42:31.380 --> 00:42:33.603
but I'll one thing about this.

00:42:34.690 --> 00:42:37.080
American members of the
audience might be familiar

00:42:37.080 --> 00:42:42.080
with Thomas Noble's
really famous painting,

00:42:43.080 --> 00:42:45.263
just before the Civil War,

00:42:47.940 --> 00:42:50.240
depicting a runaway
slave, Margaret Garner,

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:54.540
who was captured and
killed her two children

00:42:54.540 --> 00:42:58.030
so that they would not
be returned to slavery.

00:42:58.030 --> 00:43:00.660
And Thomas Noble, this was
a great abolitionist cause

00:43:00.660 --> 00:43:01.760
in the United States,

00:43:01.760 --> 00:43:05.410
and Thomas Garner made a
painting that was printed,

00:43:05.410 --> 00:43:06.470
and that was circulated

00:43:06.470 --> 00:43:08.500
in popular magazines like "Harper's,"

00:43:08.500 --> 00:43:11.760
and really was a key image for abolition.

00:43:11.760 --> 00:43:16.760
And he titled that painting
"A Modern Day Medea."

00:43:17.940 --> 00:43:19.523
And Garner was an abolitionist.

00:43:22.362 --> 00:43:23.760
That was not a shock

00:43:24.800 --> 00:43:27.770
at her having killed her
children, at her infanticide,

00:43:27.770 --> 00:43:29.200
that was an acknowledgement

00:43:29.200 --> 00:43:34.200
of the disgusting moral
conundrum that she was placed in.

00:43:34.650 --> 00:43:39.650
So we can measure in some ways
what Medea means in society,

00:43:42.440 --> 00:43:44.710
but I'm not sure I could
put a science to that

00:43:44.710 --> 00:43:46.730
in the way that I think
the question is asking me.

00:43:46.730 --> 00:43:49.280
I need to think about that a
little bit more. Thank you.

00:43:49.280 --> 00:43:50.610
- So this is a question

00:43:50.610 --> 00:43:54.180
from my ignorance of classical
theater, but is it true,

00:43:54.180 --> 00:43:56.130
you were showing the Corneille images

00:43:56.130 --> 00:43:59.603
where there's actually
violence on the stage, so,

00:43:59.603 --> 00:44:04.170
does Medea manage to
unsettle the bienseance

00:44:04.170 --> 00:44:06.010
of no violence onstage,

00:44:06.010 --> 00:44:09.773
just because you can't really,
you can't do it offstage?

00:44:11.300 --> 00:44:13.700
- You know, I'm going to say she doesn't.

00:44:13.700 --> 00:44:15.267
So, "Medee" is actually performed,

00:44:15.267 --> 00:44:18.480
"Medee" is the tragedy that
happens before "Le Cid,"

00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:21.030
and "Le Cid" is that play

00:44:21.950 --> 00:44:24.620
that generates that whole
discussion about bienseance,

00:44:24.620 --> 00:44:27.010
and out of which
bienseance kind of emerges.

00:44:27.010 --> 00:44:30.130
However, there is, for the insiders,

00:44:30.130 --> 00:44:32.180
for the 17th century insiders amongst us,

00:44:32.180 --> 00:44:35.270
I'll just say that what
Professor Kaplan's referring to

00:44:35.270 --> 00:44:40.120
is this huge debate in
the early mid 17th century

00:44:40.120 --> 00:44:42.647
about what's possible to perform onstage,

00:44:42.647 --> 00:44:45.000
and this notion of bienseance,

00:44:45.000 --> 00:44:48.240
propriety is one way of
translating that, came up,

00:44:48.240 --> 00:44:52.050
and violence onstage is just unseemly,

00:44:52.050 --> 00:44:53.230
we can't do it.

00:44:53.230 --> 00:44:56.920
And Corneille says, "Do
you know what is seemly?

00:44:56.920 --> 00:44:58.440
It's what the audience wants."

00:44:58.440 --> 00:44:59.920
He really believes

00:44:59.920 --> 00:45:01.790
that we should be listening
to what audiences want,

00:45:01.790 --> 00:45:03.240
and not telling them.

00:45:03.240 --> 00:45:06.030
And in those pamphlet wars that occur

00:45:06.030 --> 00:45:08.030
around this other play, "Le Cid,"

00:45:09.330 --> 00:45:14.040
Corneille prints for the
first time his "Medee."

00:45:14.040 --> 00:45:15.980
1639, he prints it.

00:45:15.980 --> 00:45:20.423
And so he launches "Medee" as
a firebomb into those debates.

00:45:22.360 --> 00:45:27.360
I do think that the
outcome of those debates

00:45:28.580 --> 00:45:32.550
was a gradual normative evacuation

00:45:32.550 --> 00:45:35.030
of the passions and
violence from the stage,

00:45:35.030 --> 00:45:38.970
but what replaced it was
incredibly vivid descriptions

00:45:38.970 --> 00:45:41.823
that are quite disgusting
if you read them carefully.

00:45:43.370 --> 00:45:46.180
And so in some ways there
is this transmutation

00:45:46.180 --> 00:45:50.510
of hypotyposis, of vivid imagery,
on the 17th century stage

00:45:51.800 --> 00:45:53.380
that's quite breathtaking.

00:45:53.380 --> 00:45:55.050
So I don't think that
violence ever goes away,

00:45:55.050 --> 00:45:56.470
I just don't think it's possible.

00:45:56.470 --> 00:46:01.190
- Yeah, I mean, it's a
wonderful issue that goes forth

00:46:01.190 --> 00:46:03.632
into modern literature

00:46:03.632 --> 00:46:05.990
with the whole question of restraint and,

00:46:05.990 --> 00:46:07.530
you know, ellipsis,

00:46:07.530 --> 00:46:11.030
and why you don't see Julien
Sorel at the guillotine

00:46:11.030 --> 00:46:12.060
and things like that.

00:46:12.060 --> 00:46:13.360
It's wonderful.

00:46:13.360 --> 00:46:18.360
Okay, questions are coming
faster and faster, here's one.

00:46:18.597 --> 00:46:21.310
"Are we reinterpreting Medea's story

00:46:21.310 --> 00:46:23.640
in the context of the modern era,

00:46:23.640 --> 00:46:26.040
or are we rediscovering themes

00:46:26.040 --> 00:46:30.117
that would've been more
obvious to ancient readers?"

00:46:31.750 --> 00:46:32.953
- Oh, that is-

00:46:34.374 --> 00:46:35.660
- I'm not sure what "we" means.

00:46:35.660 --> 00:46:39.143
Is, I don't know, "we" in the context of-

00:46:40.020 --> 00:46:44.460
- So I'll say what I am doing.

00:46:44.460 --> 00:46:48.850
In part, I think I'm tracing how this myth

00:46:48.850 --> 00:46:51.920
has changed in its apprehension over time.

00:46:51.920 --> 00:46:54.230
So in part, I'm trying to rediscover

00:46:54.230 --> 00:46:56.650
the ways in which these myths

00:46:56.650 --> 00:46:59.380
have a kind of sedimented construction,

00:46:59.380 --> 00:47:03.210
so that when we call somebody
today a modern day Medea,

00:47:03.210 --> 00:47:06.690
as we did Andrea Yates in the early 2000s,

00:47:06.690 --> 00:47:10.070
that's the woman who was diagnosed with,

00:47:10.070 --> 00:47:14.910
I think she was diagnosed
with psychosis, in Texas,

00:47:14.910 --> 00:47:18.260
and whose family believed
that her most important duty

00:47:18.260 --> 00:47:21.130
was to have children, so
she had five children,

00:47:21.130 --> 00:47:23.530
even though her medical doctor

00:47:23.530 --> 00:47:25.430
said that that was not
a great choice for her,

00:47:25.430 --> 00:47:28.040
and she drowned them all in the bathtub.

00:47:28.040 --> 00:47:31.800
And she was called a modern day Medea.

00:47:31.800 --> 00:47:34.303
What I'm really interested in there is,

00:47:35.840 --> 00:47:40.840
does that not seem to imply
that Medea is premodern,

00:47:40.860 --> 00:47:43.090
or that we are no longer barbaric,

00:47:43.090 --> 00:47:44.730
we no longer kill our children,

00:47:44.730 --> 00:47:47.420
but people before killed their children?

00:47:47.420 --> 00:47:50.410
And yet infanticide is a component,

00:47:50.410 --> 00:47:53.960
a feature of our society as it
has been a feature of society

00:47:53.960 --> 00:47:56.520
in different dimensions and
in different ways per society.

00:47:56.520 --> 00:47:58.100
So that's how I would say,

00:47:58.100 --> 00:48:00.450
is that we're sort of
doing both of those things.

00:48:00.450 --> 00:48:03.850
And I'm asking you to think
about the ways in which

00:48:06.500 --> 00:48:10.290
you believe that that
pushing violence out there

00:48:10.290 --> 00:48:12.330
is the right way to treat violence,

00:48:12.330 --> 00:48:13.410
when it always comes back.

00:48:13.410 --> 00:48:14.850
Is there another way to treat it,

00:48:14.850 --> 00:48:16.350
would be my sort of understanding

00:48:16.350 --> 00:48:17.680
of how we should think about that today.

00:48:17.680 --> 00:48:19.870
- So you're not giving us a manifesto,

00:48:19.870 --> 00:48:23.520
but you are saying we can
learn a lot from Medea.

00:48:23.520 --> 00:48:25.361
- I am. I am.

00:48:25.361 --> 00:48:26.220
(Juliette laughs)
- Yeah.

00:48:26.220 --> 00:48:27.960
I'm sold, I'm sold.

00:48:27.960 --> 00:48:29.670
- [Juliette] Thank you! (laughs)

00:48:29.670 --> 00:48:31.017
- Okay, next question.

00:48:31.017 --> 00:48:33.297
"Fantastic talk."

00:48:33.297 --> 00:48:34.130
- Thank you.

00:48:34.130 --> 00:48:37.940
- "Was Medea a big deal onstage
in other national contexts

00:48:37.940 --> 00:48:41.270
in the 17th century, England
or Spain, for example,

00:48:41.270 --> 00:48:46.047
or was it a particularly French
obsession, and if so, why?"

00:48:50.020 --> 00:48:53.680
- I am so sorry that I
am not as well-versed

00:48:53.680 --> 00:48:55.960
in that question as I wish I were.

00:48:55.960 --> 00:49:00.750
I do know that there are
"Medeas" in the Italian,

00:49:00.750 --> 00:49:02.890
and there are "Medeas" in Spanish,

00:49:02.890 --> 00:49:05.830
and I'm embarrassed to say
that I know very little

00:49:05.830 --> 00:49:09.860
about English tragedy

00:49:09.860 --> 00:49:11.380
other than what was given to me

00:49:11.380 --> 00:49:13.833
in the New York public school system.

00:49:15.131 --> 00:49:19.240
(laughing) And I don't know, though,

00:49:19.240 --> 00:49:21.970
the question asks, "the
obsession in France,"

00:49:21.970 --> 00:49:24.250
I don't think France was obsessed with it,

00:49:24.250 --> 00:49:28.040
and I think we can trace
what I call a Medean violence

00:49:29.350 --> 00:49:30.300
in any culture.

00:49:30.300 --> 00:49:35.020
And that is to say, attempts
at eliminating violence

00:49:35.020 --> 00:49:39.450
that then transmute that
violence into another quality

00:49:39.450 --> 00:49:41.540
to allow that violence to return

00:49:41.540 --> 00:49:44.620
and have a certain kind
of effect on society.

00:49:44.620 --> 00:49:47.290
So I'm gonna hazard a guess

00:49:50.339 --> 00:49:51.172
to that person

00:49:52.225 --> 00:49:55.640
that if the theme of Medea is not there,

00:49:55.640 --> 00:49:58.670
we could trace a Medean violence.

00:49:58.670 --> 00:50:02.500
I wish there were an expert
in any of those traditions

00:50:02.500 --> 00:50:05.214
in attendance who could tell us.

00:50:05.214 --> 00:50:06.297
That's how I
- Yeah,

00:50:06.297 --> 00:50:07.130
- would answer that.
- I'm looking.

00:50:07.130 --> 00:50:10.890
Q and A folks, please write in.

00:50:10.890 --> 00:50:11.990
Maybe you could say a little bit

00:50:11.990 --> 00:50:15.420
about these modern Medea representations.

00:50:15.420 --> 00:50:18.990
So you mentioned the fantastic Pasolini,

00:50:18.990 --> 00:50:21.433
and you showed us an
image of Maria Callas.

00:50:22.660 --> 00:50:25.600
How do they express,

00:50:25.600 --> 00:50:28.323
I mean, how do they speak
to you, those two figures?

00:50:32.350 --> 00:50:35.717
- So I will say that the Sophie Deraspe,

00:50:35.717 --> 00:50:37.400
but I'm just gonna talk
about the "Antigone,"

00:50:37.400 --> 00:50:40.870
because I've been Antigone-bashing
for quite a bit here,

00:50:40.870 --> 00:50:45.870
but I want to recover Sophie
Deraspe's "Antigone" from 2019,

00:50:46.240 --> 00:50:49.308
which does everything I say

00:50:49.308 --> 00:50:51.770
that our use of Antigone doesn't do,

00:50:51.770 --> 00:50:54.172
that the Antigone effect occludes.

00:50:54.172 --> 00:50:56.433
Deraspe's Antigone is an immigrant.

00:50:57.790 --> 00:51:00.663
It's a Quebecois film, she's an immigrant.

00:51:03.828 --> 00:51:04.878
She is working class,

00:51:06.210 --> 00:51:08.830
and she takes on an entire system

00:51:08.830 --> 00:51:10.180
in the name of her brother.

00:51:13.349 --> 00:51:14.690
The hoops are not big

00:51:14.690 --> 00:51:17.523
that one has to jump
through to imagine that.

00:51:18.430 --> 00:51:20.470
And so I'm not saying that
a figure like Antigone

00:51:20.470 --> 00:51:24.570
is not recoverable or
could not be transformed.

00:51:24.570 --> 00:51:26.640
What I'm saying is what we
usually do with Antigone

00:51:26.640 --> 00:51:28.150
is something that's very different,

00:51:28.150 --> 00:51:32.293
by focusing mostly on
her contestatory words.

00:51:33.650 --> 00:51:35.280
The Pasolini movie

00:51:36.670 --> 00:51:40.260
I think is really
remarkable because it also

00:51:40.260 --> 00:51:44.360
strives to consider how
important history is

00:51:44.360 --> 00:51:45.840
and how important knowledge is.

00:51:45.840 --> 00:51:49.170
So one of the things I've
tried to insist on in this talk

00:51:51.713 --> 00:51:54.510
is that I think that the
foreigner is a foreigner

00:51:54.510 --> 00:51:55.940
because of what they know

00:51:55.940 --> 00:51:59.040
and how their knowledge
is scary and potent

00:51:59.040 --> 00:52:00.720
and not controllable,

00:52:00.720 --> 00:52:03.840
and Pasolini does a wonderful job

00:52:05.280 --> 00:52:09.080
of putting those tensions into play,

00:52:09.080 --> 00:52:14.080
including in his
consideration of how history

00:52:14.170 --> 00:52:16.710
works on children and adults

00:52:16.710 --> 00:52:19.323
and how lessons are transmitted,

00:52:20.412 --> 00:52:22.650
transferred to generation to generation.

00:52:22.650 --> 00:52:24.970
The other thing I'll say
about Pasolini's "Medea,"

00:52:24.970 --> 00:52:27.530
if you haven't seen it, you
should run and go see it,

00:52:27.530 --> 00:52:31.270
because it's Maria Callas
who's playing Medea,

00:52:31.270 --> 00:52:33.070
and she is silent.

00:52:33.070 --> 00:52:38.070
The diva of opera is
silent for like 13 minutes.

00:52:38.810 --> 00:52:42.530
The first 13 minutes of the
film, you hear her say nothing.

00:52:42.530 --> 00:52:44.070
I can't remember, it's not 13 minutes,

00:52:44.070 --> 00:52:45.447
I can't remember how long it is,

00:52:45.447 --> 00:52:47.650
but for an incredibly
long part of this film,

00:52:47.650 --> 00:52:48.940
she says absolutely nothing.

00:52:48.940 --> 00:52:50.140
And the film begins with

00:52:52.942 --> 00:52:56.350
these atonal music that
is in such contrast

00:52:56.350 --> 00:52:58.300
to who we think we see.

00:52:58.300 --> 00:52:59.250
The other thing is that

00:52:59.250 --> 00:53:03.300
I think Callas was in her
mid 30s, so absolutely aged,

00:53:03.300 --> 00:53:08.300
and this Medea is offered
as this aged person, and-

00:53:10.057 --> 00:53:11.690
- The aged witch.

00:53:11.690 --> 00:53:15.183
- The aged witch, and I think
that that's really important.

00:53:16.190 --> 00:53:19.763
So I think that that's
quite, quite remarkable.

00:53:21.110 --> 00:53:23.610
I could name a few other
really remarkable "Medeas."

00:53:23.610 --> 00:53:28.500
- Yeah, give us some suggestions.

00:53:28.500 --> 00:53:29.333
- Okay.

00:53:31.250 --> 00:53:34.800
So, I will say

00:53:36.330 --> 00:53:38.580
that the Deborah Warner "Medea"

00:53:38.580 --> 00:53:40.390
that was starring Fiona Shaw

00:53:40.390 --> 00:53:44.390
is probably one of the best
known contemporary productions,

00:53:44.390 --> 00:53:48.690
and it is, the acting is sublime,

00:53:48.690 --> 00:53:52.840
and I think it's a
terrible interpretation.

00:53:52.840 --> 00:53:55.830
It's a beautiful play,
it's a beautiful play.

00:53:55.830 --> 00:53:58.200
I cried, I absolutely loved,

00:53:58.200 --> 00:54:00.390
it's a wonderful production,

00:54:00.390 --> 00:54:02.280
but in an interview,

00:54:02.280 --> 00:54:04.670
Fiona Shaw and Deborah Warner both said

00:54:04.670 --> 00:54:07.053
that "Medea" is a play about divorce,

00:54:07.890 --> 00:54:08.910
which is very interesting.

00:54:08.910 --> 00:54:11.400
If you read the "Medea"
play, it's about, you know,

00:54:11.400 --> 00:54:12.770
it's possible to think about it

00:54:12.770 --> 00:54:15.300
as couple folks splitting up,

00:54:15.300 --> 00:54:18.280
and so they're gonna argue
about the kids and...

00:54:19.810 --> 00:54:20.643
- [Alice] Yeah.

00:54:20.643 --> 00:54:21.476
- And that's it.

00:54:21.476 --> 00:54:24.540
But I think that what that really occludes

00:54:24.540 --> 00:54:29.540
is the asymmetry in the play
between Jason and Medea,

00:54:30.050 --> 00:54:34.710
and Warner's play does think about that,

00:54:34.710 --> 00:54:39.160
but it really evacuates the
political contestation, and-

00:54:39.160 --> 00:54:41.727
- So it becomes like Kramer and Kramer.

00:54:41.727 --> 00:54:43.710
- "Kramer vs. Kramer," right.

00:54:43.710 --> 00:54:45.540
And I don't wanna, I don't,

00:54:45.540 --> 00:54:48.950
I wanna cool how phenomenal
that production was

00:54:48.950 --> 00:54:53.040
with a difference I have
in its interpretation,

00:54:53.040 --> 00:54:55.560
that if you want to see "Medea"
as a play about divorce,

00:54:55.560 --> 00:54:58.040
that is a phenomenal play.

00:54:58.040 --> 00:55:01.840
And I'm thinking, I'm sort
of blanking, I should've-

00:55:01.840 --> 00:55:03.070
- No, no, that's okay.

00:55:04.190 --> 00:55:08.853
- There's a Rachel Cusk
version of it in which,

00:55:10.300 --> 00:55:13.700
which is very, very controversial,

00:55:13.700 --> 00:55:16.150
I think was that one at the
Court Theatre in London,

00:55:16.150 --> 00:55:17.700
which is very controversial,

00:55:17.700 --> 00:55:22.700
in which Medea doesn't kill
her children, they overdose.

00:55:23.210 --> 00:55:26.470
And this was seen as incredible violence

00:55:27.780 --> 00:55:29.313
to the Euripides.

00:55:30.260 --> 00:55:35.260
And I do, I do think that we
should maybe stop policing

00:55:35.770 --> 00:55:39.650
our interpretations of
classical tragedies,

00:55:39.650 --> 00:55:40.810
because I think that

00:55:41.930 --> 00:55:43.800
that is one thing about
classical tragedies,

00:55:43.800 --> 00:55:47.100
is that they are speaking to the problems,

00:55:47.100 --> 00:55:48.980
the intractable problems

00:55:51.630 --> 00:55:54.910
of life in this world with other people.

00:55:54.910 --> 00:55:56.260
I think that that is

00:55:57.210 --> 00:55:59.190
one of the most powerful
things about that.

00:55:59.190 --> 00:56:01.860
So a reinvention of any of these tragedies

00:56:01.860 --> 00:56:03.390
is always a good thing.

00:56:03.390 --> 00:56:04.712
- Always welcome.

00:56:04.712 --> 00:56:05.803
- Yes, always welcome.

00:56:05.803 --> 00:56:08.963
- Okay, I think we have
time for one more question.

00:56:10.209 --> 00:56:12.800
Let's see, "I understand why
the killing of her children

00:56:12.800 --> 00:56:15.760
is the episode that has stuck
in our collective minds,

00:56:15.760 --> 00:56:19.010
but were all her other
gruesome means of violence,

00:56:19.010 --> 00:56:22.690
the burning, the herbs, the
poisoned cloak, and more

00:56:22.690 --> 00:56:26.007
components of the 17th
century staged versions?"

00:56:28.460 --> 00:56:30.900
- So I would just like to say one thing,

00:56:30.900 --> 00:56:34.960
that the killing of the
children is something

00:56:34.960 --> 00:56:38.787
that happens in the ancient
Greek version of it,

00:56:38.787 --> 00:56:41.620
and that's why we have it,
that's that innovation.

00:56:41.620 --> 00:56:46.043
Before that, classicist scholars tell me,

00:56:47.050 --> 00:56:49.760
it is the Corinthian
women, or the Corinthians,

00:56:49.760 --> 00:56:52.590
who kill the children
and blame it on Medea.

00:56:52.590 --> 00:56:57.590
So part of the tragedy is to
make Medea kill her children.

00:56:57.650 --> 00:56:58.600
- Oh, wow.

00:56:58.600 --> 00:57:02.480
- To concentrate that
violence into one character.

00:57:02.480 --> 00:57:04.530
So I think that that's really important.

00:57:04.530 --> 00:57:06.220
Her other gruesome means of violence,

00:57:06.220 --> 00:57:08.150
the burning, the herbs,
the poisoned cloak,

00:57:08.150 --> 00:57:09.180
and the other components

00:57:09.180 --> 00:57:12.860
are very much part of the
17th century staged version,

00:57:12.860 --> 00:57:16.340
and in fact, Pierre Corneille
spends a lot of time

00:57:16.340 --> 00:57:18.670
really trying, and he
says this in his preface,

00:57:18.670 --> 00:57:23.670
that he really wants
to even out the guilt,

00:57:24.180 --> 00:57:27.250
even out the burden among
the different characters,

00:57:27.250 --> 00:57:31.403
and in his version, Creusa is this,

00:57:32.362 --> 00:57:36.960
she's avide, she's
materialistic, she's grabby.

00:57:36.960 --> 00:57:38.787
She asks for the coat, and she says,

00:57:38.787 --> 00:57:40.530
"You know what I'd really like?

00:57:40.530 --> 00:57:42.630
I'll spare the children's lives

00:57:42.630 --> 00:57:45.623
if you get me her coat," to Jason.

00:57:46.474 --> 00:57:49.570
And she asks for it,
and Medea says, "Okay.

00:57:49.570 --> 00:57:50.500
You want my coat?

00:57:50.500 --> 00:57:53.350
I will give you my coat,
and I will give you more."

00:57:53.350 --> 00:57:57.780
And I have an argument in
the book about that coat,

00:57:57.780 --> 00:57:59.310
but there's a way in which

00:57:59.310 --> 00:58:02.210
Corneille actually wants
to distribute blame.

00:58:02.210 --> 00:58:06.450
He's not interested in

00:58:06.450 --> 00:58:10.720
concentrating all of that
into the Medea figure,

00:58:10.720 --> 00:58:13.393
which I find really
interesting and really telling.

00:58:14.670 --> 00:58:17.720
The violence and the
poisoned coat and the burning

00:58:17.720 --> 00:58:20.420
are all part of most myths,

00:58:20.420 --> 00:58:22.953
and one thing I'll say for folks
who are interested in this,

00:58:22.953 --> 00:58:24.460
just one little thing,

00:58:24.460 --> 00:58:28.410
is that in the premodern period,

00:58:28.410 --> 00:58:30.880
there are two Medea versions.

00:58:30.880 --> 00:58:34.610
There's the Medea who burns down Corinth

00:58:34.610 --> 00:58:37.683
and kills her husband's new fiancee,

00:58:38.700 --> 00:58:41.410
burns down the city, rather,
kills her husband's fiancee,

00:58:41.410 --> 00:58:42.340
and rides off,

00:58:42.340 --> 00:58:44.020
but there's also the passionate one,

00:58:44.020 --> 00:58:46.703
the one who helps Jason
take the Golden Fleece.

00:58:46.703 --> 00:58:48.047
And often-
- Yeah, the love affair.

00:58:48.047 --> 00:58:49.420
- The love, the love.

00:58:49.420 --> 00:58:53.820
And you see those on cassone,
on Italian dowry chests,

00:58:53.820 --> 00:58:56.820
you see Jason and the Golden Fleece,

00:58:56.820 --> 00:58:58.400
Jason and Medea and the Golden Fleece

00:58:58.400 --> 00:59:00.520
on wedding dowry chests,

00:59:00.520 --> 00:59:03.920
and you see that in frescoes
celebrating marriages.

00:59:03.920 --> 00:59:05.970
And in the premodern period,

00:59:05.970 --> 00:59:09.470
there's no contradiction
between those two,

00:59:09.470 --> 00:59:12.190
those two versions of Medea
can be held simultaneously.

00:59:12.190 --> 00:59:14.021
That comes from Ovid, by the way though,

00:59:14.021 --> 00:59:14.854
that's from Ovid.
- That's very interesting.

00:59:14.854 --> 00:59:17.440
I think that's a great way to end

00:59:17.440 --> 00:59:21.250
and whet our appetite for
reading "In the Wake of Medea:

00:59:21.250 --> 00:59:24.073
Tragedy and the Arts of Destruction
in Neoclassical France."

00:59:24.073 --> 00:59:25.640
That was fantastic.

00:59:25.640 --> 00:59:27.423
Thank you very much.

00:59:28.360 --> 00:59:29.193
- Thank you all.

00:59:29.193 --> 00:59:30.210
Thank you for coming.

00:59:30.210 --> 00:59:33.023
And thank you so much for
this great opportunity.

